In accordance with an exemplary embodiment of the present disclosure, the suture-guide apparatus for use in open-heart surgical procedures comprises four primary structural components: 

**(i) an annular base member [102]** formed of a biocompatible rigid polymer, defining a central working aperture and having a anatomically contoured distal tissue-contacting face configured to conformably seat along a targeted cardiac valve annulus; 

**(ii) a plurality of angled suture-guiding channels [104]** circumferentially disposed through the body of the annular base member [102], each channel defining a convergent proximal lead-in lumen that terminates in a fixed-trajectory distal exit orifice engineered to precisely route a curved surgical needle into adjacent myocardium tissue at a predetermined vector; 

**(iii) a circumferential suture-retention ring [106]** mounted to a proximal surface of the annular base member [102] and comprising a plurality of radially spaced, elastomeric slitted friction cleats positioned adjacent to each channel to individually wedge, organize, and maintain tension on trailing suture strands; and 

**(iv) an elongated malleable positioning stem [108]** detachably connected to a central connector hub [110] extending from the annular base member [102] via a spring-biased quick-release latching mechanism, wherein the positioning stem [108] exhibits plastically deformable multi-axial bendability to facilitate spatial manipulation and line-of-sight orientation of the apparatus within a deep median sternotomy field.
